{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the implementation and tests are largely sound, but two pieces of code that implement parts of this requirement are not marked `{impl}` for it. Under the review criteria that is a gap.**\n\n**What is implemented and tested well**\n- **Grammars and profile selection.** Pinned tree-sitter grammars are in `Cargo.toml` (`tree-sitter = \"=0.27.1\"` and the exact-versioned language crates). `profile_for_path` in `src/outline/profile.rs` selects a profile by extension and falls back to `plain::PROFILE`. `profiles()` lists the Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML profiles.\n- **Plain text.** `plain_text` in `src/outline/mod.rs` makes one anonymous `line` node per non-blank line. `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` and the `outline` tool test cover it.\n- **Kind and name tables.** Each profile has a `KindRule` table with `NameRule` variants: `Anonymous`, `Field`, `FirstChild` or `Custom`. These are the per-language tables for Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML. The Rust table is discussed under the gaps below.\n- **Transparency, root and comments.**\n  - `Builder::walk` makes unmapped nodes transparent.\n  - `maps()` excludes comments from the outline.\n  - `root_node` gives every file a root with no invented fallback kinds.\n  - `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` and `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` test these three points.\n- **Attached siblings.** Attached siblings are handled by the `attaches` hook (Rust attributes and outer doc comments, Python decorators). The tools test (`/// Docs.` attached to `struct Thing`, lines 1–2) and `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements` (`def[f] 2-5`) cover it.\n- **Profile tests.** There are per-profile tests for Python, TypeScript and TSX, CSS, Markdown, and TOML and YAML. `reports_parse_failures_with_lines` covers the failure path.\n- **Documentation.** The profiles are documented in `md/reference.md` (the \"Outlines\" and \"Outline profiles\" sections, which cite this requirement). The table matches the code. The document states that grammars are pinned and that upgrades are deliberate.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Attached-sibling line ranges are not marked for this requirement.** The requirement says attached siblings \"are part of that node for its line range and hash\". `Builder::finish` in `src/outline/mod.rs` computes `line_start` as the minimum over `draft.attached` and the core node. That is the line-range half of the obligation. `finish` is marked only `requirements.references.outline_selectors`, not `requirements.references.outlines`.\n2. **Markdown's `is_comment` is not marked for this requirement.** In `src/outline/markdown.rs` it carries only `outline_comments`. It is the code that makes HTML-comment blocks non-nodes in that profile, which implements \"Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes\". The `is_comment` functions in the CSS and Rust profiles are marked `outlines`. Markdown's is not.\n3. **Rust's kind table is marked only for the refining requirement.** The Rust `KINDS`, `impl_name`, `extern_name`, `in_item_list` and `item_macro` are marked only `rust_item_selectors`. That requirement refines this one, so this is probably acceptable. It is inconsistent with the other languages' `KINDS`, which are marked `outlines`.\n\n**Minor test thinness**\n- No test exercises the less common extensions (`.pyi`, `.mts`, `.cts`, `.markdown`, `.yaml`) or case-insensitive extension matching.\n- No test checks that the grammar versions are pinned.\n\nNone of these gaps is acknowledged by a FIXME in source, so the judgment is `false` rather than FIXME. Marking `finish` (line ranges) and Markdown's `is_comment` with `{impl}` for `requirements.references.outlines` would close the main gaps.",
  "inputs": [
